{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>He who walks with the wise shall grow wise, and he who tends fools shall be broken (Proverbs 13:20).</b>" "<b>King Solomon</b>, peace be upon him, warns in this verse (Proverbs 13) that a person should strive for the company of the wise and distance himself from the company of fools. And he says: 'he who walks' — who walks after them always and joins them and makes himself subordinate to them, and sets them as the heads over him — one who has this trait will without doubt grow wise. And one who joins with fools, so that he becomes head over them, 'shall be broken' (ירוע), that is, he shall be crushed. And so the verse makes clear the harm that comes from the company of fools and the benefit that comes to a person from the company of the wise: for this one is crushed and that one grows wise.\n\nAnd know that one who joins with the wise benefits, and the wise man loses nothing at all. Therefore the Torah was likened to a lamp, in the manner that David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119:105): ״נר לרגלי דברך ואור לנתיבתי״ — because from a lamp everyone lights and its light loses nothing. And from this they said in the Midrash: 'he who walks with the wise shall grow wise' — to what may the matter be compared? To a person who entered a perfumer's shop and absorbed the fragrance of the perfumes; when he left, all his garments were perfumed, though he bought nothing of the perfume — that is, and the perfumer sold him nothing. And this is what is said: 'he who walks with the wise shall grow wise, and he who tends fools shall be broken.' He means that one who joins with fools shall be broken, that is, the matter will come to great harm, for one who joins with them harms himself, and harm and loss will also come to them. And so he is the opposite of one who joins with the wise. And what emerges for us from this is that there is harm in the company of fools without benefit, and there is benefit in the company of the wise without harm.\n\nAnd the term 'fools' (כסילים) refers to those who choose base traits and bodily lusts and despise matters of the intellect. And they are the ones who are not fit to be reproved, because they are the group of the three groups that Solomon, peace be upon him, commanded not to reprove. And these are they: fools (כסילים), scoffers (לצים), and wicked men (רשעים). 'Fools' — as it says (Proverbs 23:9): ״באזני כסיל אל תדבר כי יבוז לשכל מליך״. 'Scoffers' — as it says (Proverbs 9:8): ״אל תוכח לץ פן ישנאך״. 'Wicked men' — as it says (ibid.): ״יוסר לץ לוקח לו קלון ומוכיח לרשע מומו״.\n\nAnd just as the company of the wise is a great cause and a strong preparation for fulfilling the service of the Holy One, blessed be He, and for persevering in it, so the company of fools and the wicked is a great preparation and a cause for its neglect. For thus it is written (Deuteronomy 7:4): ״כי יסיר את בנך מאחרי״. Therefore David, peace be upon him, saw fit to begin his book with the distancing of the company of the wicked, and immediately mentioned the matter of Torah — as he said (Psalms 1:1): ״אשרי האיש אשר לא הלך בעצת רשעים ובדרך חטאים וגו׳״, and he immediately appended: ״כי אם בתורת ה׳ חפצו״.\n\nAnd so Solomon, peace be upon him, took this very path himself, for he began the first of his words with the matter of Torah — as he said (Proverbs 1:8): ״שמע בני מוסר אביך וגו׳״ — and immediately appended the things that prevent it, namely the company of the wicked — as he said (ibid.): ״בני אם יפתוך חטאים אל תבא״.\n\nAnd so Abraham our father, peace be upon him, wished to fulfill the matter of Torah and divine service — and as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'Abraham our father fulfilled even the matter of ערובי תבשילין' — and he needed to beware of the company of the wicked men of his generation, who were a cause of nullifying free choice. Therefore the word of the Holy One, blessed be He, came to him, that he should distance himself from them and not intermingle with them, and that he should go from his land and his birthplace to the land that He would show him. And this is what is written: ״ויאמר ה׳ אל אברם לך לך מארצך וממולדך ומבית אביך אל הארץ אשר אראך״.\n\nAnd the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: Abraham was like a flask of spiced perfume placed in a graveyard, whose fragrance was not known; they took it and moved it from its place, and it made its fragrance known to the world. So was Abraham dwelling among idolaters. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: לך לך — go for yourself, distance yourself from them and do not intermingle with them. And this is the intent of the expression לך לך.\n\nAnd know that this is one of the ten trials with which Abraham was tried, and he stood firm through them all. And this is the first, and the last is the matter of the binding (עקדה), and it is written in this very expression: ״ולך לך אל ארץ המוריה״ — in order to equate the last with the first. And this utterance was in Haran, for it is his land and his birthplace, and concerning it Abraham said (Genesis 24:38): ״אם לא אל בית אבי תלך וגו׳״.\n\nAnd without doubt this was a great trial — that he should go from his land and his birthplace where he was born and where he had dwelt all along, and where all his family and his father's house were, to a land he did not know.\n\nAnd know that before Scripture mentions ״ויאמר ה׳ אל אברם״, it would have been fitting to inform us of his virtues and his traits, so that we might recognize who he is, and afterward mention the utterance — for so it did with Noah. But it may be said that Scripture had already revealed to us in the preceding parashah the greatness of his virtue by way of allusion, in the word אור כשדים, as was explained there.\n\nAnd it says:"], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:12:03.706674710Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}